## The question

Can a simple forecast of Québec's hourly electricity demand be trusted, and how would you know when to stop trusting it? This project does not look for the best forecast: it checks this one the way a model-risk team would. It measures its error on months it has not seen, checks what its ranges are worth, then replays a whole year as production, under a monitor that can recommend investigating the data, recalibrating the ranges or retraining the model. The monitor recommends; it never acts. ({{ sec(1) }})

## The forecast

At the end of each day, it forecasts the next day's 24 hours from the hour and the type of day, the previous day's and the previous week's demand, and the previous day's temperature in {{ primary }}, the only one known when the forecast is issued. Each month from {{ vy[0] }} to {{ vy[1] }} is forecast by a model fitted on the hours before it only. {{ brief_error(P, c1, vy) }} With the target hour's actually observed temperature, as if the weather were perfectly forecast, the average gap would be {{ P.observed.overall.mean|pct(2) }}: an optimistic bound, which the forecast does not have. ({{ sec(2) }})

