## Is silence good news?

A silent monitor reassures only if it can see a real change. The report measured that by imposing sustained changes at a few fixed starts and replaying the real rules. On {{ r.year }}, {% if LR.every_from is not none %}a sustained gap of ±{{ LR.every_from|pct(0) }} or more between demand and forecast yielded "retrain" within {{ K.power_horizon }} weeks at each of the {{ LR.starts }} starts{% else %}even the largest gap measured between demand and forecast, ±{{ LR.sizes[-1]|pct(0) }}, did not yield "retrain" within {{ K.power_horizon }} weeks at all {{ LR.starts }} starts{% endif %}; a gap of ±{{ LR.smallest|pct(0) }} yielded it at {{ small.up.caught + small.down.caught }} of {{ small.up.cases + small.down.cases }} cases. The forecast leans on the previous day's demand: it follows a lasting rise in demand, and leaves between the two only a gap much smaller than the rise itself. A sustained {{ level.size|pct(0) }} rise yielded "retrain" within {{ K.step_horizon }} weeks at {{ level.up_n - level.up_missed }} of {{ level.up_n }} starts on {{ p.year }} (the year the rules were designed on), and at {{ level24.up_n - level24.up_missed }} of {{ level24.up_n }} on {{ r.year }} (out of sample, a measurement added after the results). {% if missed_any %}Silence is therefore no proof: sustained changes went unnoticed at some of the starts measured.{% else %}At each of the starts measured, on {{ p.year }} as on {{ r.year }}, every sustained gap and every {{ level.size|pct(0) }} rise imposed was caught; these are only a few starts.{% endif %} ({{ sec(6) }}, {{ sec(7) }})

